            <ArticleTitle>Effort-reward imbalance and low back pain among eldercare workers in nursing homes: a cross-sectional study in Kagawa Prefecture, Japan.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>197-204</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To clarify effort-reward imbalance among eldercare workers in nursing homes and to examine the association between the effort-reward imbalance (ERI) and low back pain (LBP) among them.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A total of 549 questionnaires were distributed to a random sample comprising 23% of the 79 special elderly nursing homes in Kagawa in 2013. A total of 467 eldercare workers (response rate 85%) participated in this study. Of the 467 eligible respondents, 372 (80%) completed all items of the ERI questionnaire. Complete data were available for 342 (73%) eldercare workers.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Of the 342 respondents, 215 (63%) had LBP at the time of the study, and 291 (85%) showed a critical &quot;high cost/low gain&quot; condition as determined by an effort-reward ratio &gt;1. Multiple logistic regression analyses adjusted for age, gender, previous LBP experience, high-risk work and K6 score showed that eldercare workers with a high ERI had a higher risk for LBP than workers with a low ERI (OR, 1.96; 95% CI, 1.02-3.77).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Most eldercare workers have a high ERI, and their LBP is associated with their ERI. Balancing effort and reward may be an important factor for improving LBP among eldercare workers in nursing homes.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
